==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[prognostick]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.}} {{term|prognosticus|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc}} {{term|προγνωστικός||foreknowing|tr=prognostikos|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}, from {{term|πρό-|tr=pro-|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}} + {{term|γνωστικός|tr=gnostikos||of or for knowing, good at knowing|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}, from {{term|γιγνώσκω|tr=gignosko||to learn to know, to perceive, to mark, to learn|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}.
Compare {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|pronostic||prognostic|lang=fr}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of, pertaining to or characterized by [[prognosis]] or [[prediction]].

====Synonyms====
* [[foretelling]]
* [[predictive]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{rare|medicine}} [[prognosis]]
#: {{rfquotek|Parr}}
# A [[sign]] by which a future event may be [[known]] or [[foretold]].
#: ''Careful observers may foretell the hour''
#: ''(By sure prognostics) when to dread a show’r.''
#: ''While rain depends, the pensive cat gives o’er''
#: ''Her frolics, and pursues her tail no more.'' Jonathan Swift
# A [[prediction]] of the [[future]].
#* Macaulay
#*: The choice of a successor was no light matter. That choice would inevitably be considered by the country as a '''prognostic''' of the highest import.
# One who [[predict]]s the [[future]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|sign}} [[indication]], [[sign]], [[omen]], [[foretelling]], [[prediction]]

